WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:05.590 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  spirits were high at this convention  center in sao paulo thousands gathered  to hear brazil's former president  louisiana silva as he promised to defeat  incumbent jair bolsonaro in october's  election  mulla announced that his candidacy has  the support of trade unions social  organizations and seven center-left  parties including his own workers party  he compared his two-term presidency of  latin america's largest democracy to  that of bolsonaro an admirer and  follower of former u.s president donald  trump  brazil is too big to be an international  pariah because we are submissive because  we are denialists and because we attack  our greatest commercial partners causing  enormous damage to our economy  brazil will hold general elections in  five months as the country deals with  record two-digit yearly inflation high  interest rates and unemployment  brazil is back on the united nations  hunger map which it had left in 2014 for  the first time in its history  rula also appealed to young voters  promising to protect the amazon the  environment and brazil's indigenous  people  recent polls show lula having the  support of more than 40 percent of the  population and jai bolsonaro in second  place with more than 30 percent  but lula supporters are anxious they  know the tables can turn at any moment  there is no guarantee that we will be  victorious we are very much at risk of  having brazilians re-elect bolsonaro's  fascist government this is a historic  moment when progressive forces unite to  fight evil but anything can happen  bolsonaro has the support of brazil's  powerful farm lobby of the christian  right and of brazilians who were  disappointed with lula and with the  corruption scandals involving him and  his workers party  but bolsonaro is also being accused of  incompetence and mismanagement of the  kovit-19 crisis which has killed more  than  660 000 brazilians  officially the electoral campaign in  brazil  starts on august the 15th but both lula  and the current president bolsonaro are  already fighting for every single vote  they can get in what promises to be a  very polarized election  monica yanagiv al jazeera so far

